    "created_at": "2015-08-28T14:10:16Z",
    "updated_at": "2015-08-28T14:10:16Z",
    "body": "You're embarassing me further. :) Yes, it's [the inputs section](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/forms.html#attr-input-placeholder).\n\nMy point was that I don't have a clear distinction between inputs and textareas. Yes, textarea happens to use a different tag name, but for all intents and purposes, inputs, texteareas, and selects are the same for me. I always consider those to be interactive \"inputs\" sharing many attributes (`name`, `readonly`, `placeholder`, and others), and the different tag names to be only a historical artifact. If you disagree, consider `input[type=radio]`. :) \n\nSo, that said, if I take a look at the [table of contents of the Forms](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/forms.html), I see the placeholder being mentioned only in the 4.10.5.3.10. and it's a section common for all inputs and it includes all the things I've expected to see, like `readonly`, `required`, and so on. In addition, as I said previously, if I search for \"placeholder\" on `developers.whatwg.org`, that's the section that shows up.\n\nNow I realize that the actual section that's common to all form controls is [\"Attributes common to form contorls\"](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/forms.html#attributes-common-to-form-controls), and I was mistaken. However, I do still believe that a `input[type=text]` has more in common with a `textarea` than with `input[type=radio]`. More behavior, and yes, more attributes.\n\nSo perhaps my confusion and my GitHub issue is a bit more, after all, it's about improving grouping different form elements. From their current \"by a meaningless historical element name\" to \"by their actual usage and properties\".\n",
    "author_association": "NONE",
